# BuildKit honours `<Dockerfile>.dockerignore` next to the Dockerfile, so this
# lives in the platform repo (not the build-context root). Patterns are relative
# to the build context (the parent dir holding dpp-engine/).
#
# 🚨 This is a WHITELIST, and it must stay one.
#
# It used to name the siblings to exclude. That missed every sibling nobody
# thought of — the context root also holds private repositories, and 209 MB of
# one of them was being handed to the Docker daemon on every build. Nothing
# leaked into an image, because the Dockerfile copies named directories and never
# `COPY . .`, but a build context is not the place to find that out.
#
# A denylist here fails open: a new sibling checkout is shipped by default and
# nobody notices. This form fails closed — a directory the build genuinely needs
# must be named, and anything else is simply not sent.
*
!dpp-core
!dpp-engine

# Now trim the two directories that are re-included above.
**/target
**/node_modules
**/.git
**/.github
**/dist
**/.astro
**/*.log
**/.env
**/.env.*
!**/.env.example
# Local [patch.crates-io] override — never bake the sibling-path patch into an
# image that must build from crates.io.
**/.cargo/config.toml
